---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { PUBLISHER, SITE } from '../consts';
import { simplePageBreadcrumb } from '../lib/schema';
const description = `How ${SITE.name} reports, reviews and scores, how we handle corrections and conflicts of interest, and how advertising is kept separate from editorial.`;
const url = `${SITE.url}/editorial-policy/`;
---
<Base title="Editorial Policy"
      {description} canonical={url} schema={[
        {
          '@type': 'WebPage', '@id': `${url}#webpage`, url,
          name: 'Editorial Policy', description,
          isPartOf: { '@id': `${SITE.url}/#website` },
        },
        simplePageBreadcrumb('/editorial-policy/', 'Editorial Policy'),
      ]}>
  <div class="wrap">
    <nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a> / Editorial Policy</nav>
    <div class="prose" style="padding:34px 0 10px">
      <h1>Editorial Policy</h1>
      <p class="updated">Last updated 2 October 2026.</p>

      <p>
        {SITE.name}.com is an independent publication dedicated exclusively to iOS, iPhone, iPad, Mac, watchOS, and the Apple ecosystem. This page sets out how we report, how we test software and hardware, how we correct mistakes, and how our commercial arrangements work. Questions can be directed to{' '}
        <a href={`mailto:${PUBLISHER.editorialEmail}`}>{PUBLISHER.editorialEmail}</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>What we publish</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Comprehensive iOS and iPadOS tutorials and guides</li>
        <li>iPhone battery, performance, camera, and security optimization tips</li>
        <li>Mac and macOS workflows and power-user configurations</li>
        <li>In-depth, independent reviews of iOS apps and Apple accessories</li>
        <li>Apple ecosystem updates, Continuity walkthroughs, and verified industry news</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        Every article is reviewed and approved by an editor before publication. Research and drafting tools may assist the process, but human editors are responsible for verifying technical steps, checking factual accuracy on current Apple operating systems, and approving the final publication.
      </p>

      <h2>Reporting standards</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>We prioritize verifiable testing on actual Apple hardware running supported iOS and macOS versions.</li>
        <li>Rumours and prospective supply chain leaks are clearly labelled as rumours.</li>
        <li>We write accurate, descriptive headlines that match the article content without clickbait.</li>
        <li>We prompt and transparently correct errors when software updates change behavior.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>How we review and score</h2>
      <p>
        First-person claims about testing an app, accessory, or feature are only made when hands-on evaluation took place. Review scores are editorial assessments based on usability, design quality, privacy practices, system resource footprint, and value for money:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>4.5 to 5.0</strong> &mdash; Outstanding, best-in-class Apple ecosystem experience</li>
        <li><strong>3.8 to 4.4</strong> &mdash; Highly recommended with minor caveats</li>
        <li><strong>2.5 to 3.7</strong> &mdash; Acceptable, but better alternatives exist</li>
        <li><strong>Below 2.5</strong> &mdash; Poor implementation or privacy concerns</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Corrections</h2>
      <p>
        We correct inaccuracies rather than quietly deleting them. If an Apple software update alters a procedure explained in one of our guides, please send the article URL and details to{' '}
        <a href={`mailto:${PUBLISHER.editorialEmail}`}>{PUBLISHER.editorialEmail}</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Bylines and pen names</h2>
      <p>
        {SITE.name} uses consistent editorial bylines. The <strong>{SITE.name} Editorial</strong> byline is used for work produced or reviewed collectively by our technical staff. Editorial responsibility for all content on this site rests with {PUBLISHER.legalName}. You can browse our writers on the <a href="/authors/">Authors page</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Advertising and editorial independence</h2>
      <p>
        We maintain strict boundaries between editorial evaluation and advertising. Commercial relationships never determine our scores or recommendations. If an app or accessory is substandard, we say so plainly. We do not accept payment to remove or alter negative assessments.
      </p>

      <h2>What we will not publish</h2>
      <p>
        We do not cover malware, pirated software, jailbreak tweaks that compromise device security, or illicit profile certificates that violate Apple's developer agreements and user privacy.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
